# App Store "App Review Information": sign-in (demo) account + contact for the
# reviewer. Secrets come from the environment (GitHub Actions secrets / a local
# untracked env), never hardcode them, the repo is public.
# Returns nil when the demo creds are absent so deliver skips review info
# cleanly (it early-returns on a nil app_review_information).
#
# The notes answer, in order, the seven points App Review asked for under
# Guideline 2.1 (Information Needed) on the 1.0.0 submission, and Apple wants
# them kept here for every future submission. Point 1 (the screen recording)
# comes from APPLE_REVIEW_VIDEO_URL, see kiroku_review_notes. The BAC paragraph
# is there because 0.3.22 was rejected under Guideline 1.4 (Physical Harm) as
# "marketed as a blood alcohol content calculator", which Kiroku has never
# been. ASC caps the field at 4000 characters.
KIROKU_REVIEW_NOTES = <<~NOTES.freeze
  2. PURPOSE AND AUDIENCE
  Kiroku is a personal alcohol-consumption diary for adults (18+). Users log the drinks they choose to record and see them back as totals, standard units, and alcohol-free days, in a calendar and in statistics. Most people underestimate how much they drink; seeing your own pattern makes it easier to set a daily limit, notice trends, and cut back. The app is built around harm reduction. It doesn't encourage, reward, or gamify drinking, gives no medical advice, and points users to a healthcare professional if they're concerned about their drinking.

  Kiroku does NOT estimate, calculate, or display blood alcohol content, and makes no claim about a user's level of impairment. It never asks for body weight, sex, height, or time since the last drink, and shows no intoxication readout or time-to-sober countdown. "Units" are the WHO standard-drink measure (grams of ethanol per drink), a labelling convention, not a physiological estimate. The colour of a calendar day reflects the user's own self-set daily limit.

  3. HOW TO REVIEW
  Sign in with the demo account from the Sign-In Information fields, using the email/password form on the first screen. Sign in with Apple and Google are also offered, and a new account can be created with any email. The demo account has several months of logged sessions and a few friends, so every screen has content.
  - Home tab: the calendar of past days. Tap + to start a session, log drinks, then finish it.
  - Statistics tab: totals, units over time, and alcohol-free days.
  - Friends tab: friend list, search, and requests. Open a friend's profile and tap Manage to Report or Block them.
  - Account deletion: Settings > Privacy > Manage account > Delete account. It permanently deletes the account and its data.
  - Tip jar: Settings > Support Kiroku (see 7).

  User-generated content: display names, nicknames, and profile photos are visible to a user's friends, and friends can see each other's sessions unless a user hides them in Settings > Privacy. There is no public feed and no chat or messaging. Uploaded profile photos are screened automatically for explicit content. Users can report or block a friend from that friend's profile; reports go to our moderation team, and blocked users are listed under Settings > Privacy > Blocked users.

  4. EXTERNAL SERVICES
  - Firebase (Google): Authentication (email/password, Sign in with Apple, Google Sign-In), Realtime Database and Cloud Storage for user data and profile photos, Cloud Functions for our backend API, and Crashlytics and Performance Monitoring for crash and performance reports (users can turn these off in Settings > Privacy).
  - Google Cloud Vision SafeSearch: screens uploaded profile photos for explicit content. There are no generative AI features.
  - RevenueCat, on top of Apple In-App Purchase, for the tip jar.
  - Apple Core Location, only if the user turns on Settings > Privacy > Tag drinks with location (off by default).
  No advertising or analytics SDKs.

  5. REGIONS
  The app works the same in every region; no feature or content differs by region. Tip prices are shown in each storefront's local currency, as reported by Apple.

  6. REGULATED INDUSTRY AND THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL
  Not applicable. Kiroku doesn't sell, deliver, or advertise alcohol, isn't a medical device, gives no medical advice, and contains no licensed third-party material.

  7. IN-APP PURCHASES
  Three optional consumable tips: "A small beer", "A pint", and "A round for the team" (kiroku.tipjar.small_beer, kiroku.tipjar.pint, kiroku.tipjar.round). To buy one: Settings tab > Support Kiroku > pick a tier > tap the buy button. A tip unlocks nothing: no feature, badge, or limit changes, and every feature stays free. This version has no subscriptions.
NOTES

def kiroku_review_notes
  video_url = ENV["APPLE_REVIEW_VIDEO_URL"]
  recording =
    if video_url.to_s.empty?
      "A screen recording on a physical iPhone is attached to our reply in App Review."
    else
      "Screen recording on a physical iPhone: #{video_url}"
    end

  "1. SCREEN RECORDING\n#{recording}\n\n#{KIROKU_REVIEW_NOTES}"
end

require 'open3'

# Exit codes of `node scripts/asc.mjs submit-gate`.
SUBMIT_GATE_SUBMIT = 0
SUBMIT_GATE_SKIP = 2

# Asks App Store Connect (read-only) whether the production lane may submit
# app_version for review. Returns true to submit, false to skip. Raises when
# the check itself fails: submitting blind is what the gate exists to prevent.
def kiroku_submit_gate(app_version, build_number)
  # Lanes run with cwd = ./fastlane; asc.mjs resolves the API key from the root.
  repo_root = File.basename(Dir.pwd) == "fastlane" ? File.expand_path("..") : Dir.pwd
  output, status = Open3.capture2e(
    "node", "scripts/asc.mjs", "submit-gate",
    "--version", app_version, "--build", build_number,
    chdir: repo_root
  )
  output.each_line { |line| UI.message(line.rstrip) }

  case status.exitstatus
  when SUBMIT_GATE_SUBMIT then true
  when SUBMIT_GATE_SKIP then false
  else UI.user_error!("asc.mjs submit-gate failed (exit #{status.exitstatus.inspect}), not submitting blind")
  end
rescue Errno::ENOENT
  UI.user_error!("node not found; the App Store submit gate needs it (scripts/asc.mjs submit-gate)")
end

def kiroku_review_information
  demo_user = ENV["APPLE_DEMO_EMAIL"]
  demo_password = ENV["APPLE_DEMO_PASSWORD"]
  return nil if demo_user.to_s.empty? || demo_password.to_s.empty?

  {
    first_name: ENV.fetch("APPLE_CONTACT_FIRST_NAME", "Petr"),
    last_name: ENV.fetch("APPLE_CONTACT_LAST_NAME", "Čala"),
    phone_number: ENV["APPLE_CONTACT_PHONE"],
    email_address: ENV["APPLE_CONTACT_EMAIL"],
    demo_user: demo_user,
    demo_password: demo_password,
    notes: kiroku_review_notes
  }
end

platform :android do


  desc "Generate a new local APK"
  lane :build do
    ENV["ENVFILE"]=".env.production"

    gradle(
      project_dir: './android',
      task: 'assemble',
      flavor: 'Production',
      build_type: 'Release',
    )
  end


  desc "Build Android APK for testing (no upload)"
  lane :build_android do
    ENV["ENVFILE"]=".env.adhoc"

    gradle(
      project_dir: './android',
      task: 'assemble',
      flavor: 'Adhoc',
      build_type: 'Release',
      # Ad hoc builds only ever land on arm64 phones, so building the other
      # ABIs from android/gradle.properties (armeabi-v7a, x86) is wasted native
      # compile. x86_64 stays: it's what CI/local Android emulators run (see
      # e2e/native/README.md, which installs this same ad-hoc APK via
      # reactivecircus/android-emulator-runner), and dropping it would make
      # the test build emulator-only-uninstallable for no CI-time gain, since
      # x86_64 already builds fastest of the four. Scoped to this lane only,
      # the release AAB keeps the full ABI set from gradle.properties.
      properties: {
        "reactNativeArchitectures" => "arm64-v8a,x86_64"
      },
    )

    # Persist APK path for the separate upload step (avoids AWS token expiry
    # on long cold-cache builds by letting the workflow re-assume the role).
    sh("echo '#{lane_context[SharedValues::GRADLE_APK_OUTPUT_PATH]}' > ../android_apk_path.txt")
  end

  desc "Upload built Android APK to S3 (requires fresh AWS credentials)"
  lane :upload_android_to_s3 do
    apk_path = File.read('../android_apk_path.txt').strip

    aws_s3(
      bucket: ENV['S3_BUCKET'],
      region: ENV['S3_REGION'],

      apk: apk_path,
      app_directory: "android/#{ENV['PULL_REQUEST_NUMBER']}",
    )

    sh("echo '{\"apk_path\": \"#{lane_context[SharedValues::S3_APK_OUTPUT_PATH]}\",\"html_path\": \"#{lane_context[SharedValues::S3_HTML_OUTPUT_PATH]}\"}' > ../android_paths.json")
  end

  desc "Build app for testing"
  lane :build_internal do
    build_android
    upload_android_to_s3
  end

  desc "Build Android app bundle for Google Play (no upload)"
  lane :build_beta do
    ENV["ENVFILE"]=".env.production"

    gradle(
        project_dir: './android',
        task: 'bundle',
        flavor: 'Production',
        build_type: 'Release',
    )
  end

  desc "Upload the built Android app bundle to Google Play"
  lane :upload_beta_to_play_store do
    # Google is very unreliable, so we retry a few times
    ENV["SUPPLY_UPLOAD_MAX_RETRIES"]="5"

    upload_to_play_store(
        package_name: "com.alcohol_tracker",
        json_key: './android/app/android-fastlane-json-key.json',
        aab: './android/app/build/outputs/bundle/productionRelease/app-production-release.aab',
        track: 'internal',
        rollout: '1.0',
        skip_upload_apk: true,
    )
  end

  desc "Build and upload app to Google Play"
  lane :beta do
    build_beta
    upload_beta_to_play_store
  end


  desc "Capture Play Store screenshots on phone + tablet across locales"
  lane :screenshots do
    ENV["ENVFILE"] = ".env.production"

    # Build the production APK + the androidTest APK. screengrab needs both.
    gradle(
      project_dir: './android',
      task: 'assemble',
      flavor: 'Production',
      build_type: 'Release',
    )
    gradle(
      project_dir: './android',
      task: 'assembleProductionReleaseAndroidTest',
    )

    capture_android_screenshots   # reads Screengrabfile
  end

  desc "Promote the internal build to Google Play production as a staged rollout"
  lane :production do
    # Google is very unreliable, so we retry a few times
    ENV["SUPPLY_UPLOAD_MAX_RETRIES"]="5"
    upload_to_play_store(
      package_name: "com.alcohol_tracker",
      json_key: './android/app/android-fastlane-json-key.json',
      version_code: ENV["VERSION"].to_i,
      track: 'internal',
      track_promote_to: 'production',
      # A fraction below 1 makes supply create an inProgress staged rollout:
      # the new build reaches 20% of users and the previous production release
      # keeps serving the rest. Ramp or complete it with
      # `node scripts/play.mjs promote --version-code <code> [--rollout <f>] --yes`.
      # The release still goes to Google review, and Play Console "Managed
      # publishing" holds the go-live until it's published by hand.
      rollout: '0.2',
      skip_upload_apk: true,
      skip_upload_aab: true,
      skip_upload_metadata: true,
      skip_upload_changelogs: true,
      skip_upload_images: true,
      skip_upload_screenshots: true
    )
  end
end

platform :ios do

  desc "Generate a local iOS production build"
  lane :build do
    ENV["ENVFILE"]=".env.production"

    build_app(
        workspace: "./ios/kiroku.xcworkspace",
        scheme: "Kiroku (production)"
    )
  end

  desc "Build app for testing"
    lane :build_internal do

      require 'securerandom'
      ENV["ENVFILE"]=".env.adhoc"

      keychain_password = SecureRandom.uuid

      create_keychain(
          name: "ios-build.keychain",
          password: keychain_password,
          default_keychain: "true",
          unlock: "true",
          timeout: "3600",
          add_to_search_list: "true"
      )

      import_certificate(
          certificate_path: "./ios/Certificates.p12",
          keychain_name: "ios-build.keychain",
          keychain_password: keychain_password,
          certificate_password: ENV["IOS_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD"],
      )

      install_provisioning_profile(
          path: "./ios/Kiroku_AdHoc.mobileprovision"
      )

      # The embedded watchOS app signs with its own ad-hoc profile (distinct
      # .adhoc.watchkitapp App ID, same Apple Distribution cert). Bound by name
      # in export_options below; (re)minted by scripts/ios-signing.mjs watch-setup.
      install_provisioning_profile(
          path: "./ios/KirokuWatch_AdHoc.mobileprovision"
      )

      build_app(
          workspace: "./ios/kiroku.xcworkspace",
          skip_profile_detection: true,
          scheme: "Kiroku (AdHoc)",
          export_method: "ad-hoc",
          export_options: {
              method: "ad-hoc",
              provisioningProfiles: {
                # Distinct bundle id (.adhoc suffix) so the ad-hoc build installs
                # side by side with the App Store build, mirrors Android's
                # applicationIdSuffix ".adhoc". The Kiroku_AdHoc profile is bound to
                # this id in the portal (see scripts/ios-signing.mjs adhoc-setup).
                "com.kiroku.app.adhoc" => "Kiroku_AdHoc",
                # Embedded Apple Watch app (ad-hoc).
                "com.kiroku.app.adhoc.watchkitapp" => "KirokuWatch_AdHoc",
              },
              manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber: false
          }
      )

    # A role should have been assumed before this step
    aws_s3(
      bucket: ENV['S3_BUCKET'],
      region: ENV['S3_REGION'],

      ipa: lane_context[SharedValues::IPA_OUTPUT_PATH],
      app_directory: "ios/#{ENV['PULL_REQUEST_NUMBER']}",
    )

    sh("echo '{\"ipa_path\": \"#{lane_context[SharedValues::S3_IPA_OUTPUT_PATH]}\",\"html_path\": \"#{lane_context[SharedValues::S3_HTML_OUTPUT_PATH]}\"}' > ../ios_paths.json")
  end


  desc "Build and upload app to TestFlight"
  lane :beta do
    require 'securerandom'
    ENV["ENVFILE"]=".env.production"

    keychain_password = SecureRandom.uuid

    create_keychain(
        name: "ios-build.keychain",
        password: keychain_password,
        default_keychain: "true",
        unlock: "true",
        timeout: "3600",
        add_to_search_list: "true"
    )

    import_certificate(
        certificate_path: "./ios/Certificates.p12",
        keychain_name: "ios-build.keychain",
        keychain_password: keychain_password,
        certificate_password: ENV["IOS_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD"],
    )

    install_provisioning_profile(
        path: "./ios/Kiroku.mobileprovision"
    )

    # The embedded watchOS app signs with its own App Store profile (.watchkitapp
    # App ID, same Apple Distribution cert). Bound by name in export_options below;
    # (re)minted by scripts/ios-signing.mjs watch-setup and kept fresh by `renew`.
    install_provisioning_profile(
        path: "./ios/KirokuWatch.mobileprovision"
    )

    # Xcode will handle JS bundling via bundle-react-native-code-and-images.sh (uses rock bundler)
    build_app(
        workspace: "./ios/kiroku.xcworkspace",
        scheme: "Kiroku (production)",
        output_name: "kiroku.ipa",
        export_options: {
            provisioningProfiles: {
              "com.kiroku.app" => "Kiroku",
              # Embedded Apple Watch app (App Store).
              "com.kiroku.app.watchkitapp" => "KirokuWatch",
            },
            manageAppVersionAndBuildNumber: false
        }
    )

    # App Store Connect's TestFlight ingestion (altool / the "ContentDelivery"
    # pipeline) intermittently returns a transient HTTP 500 on the final
    # "CHANGE UPLOAD STATE TO COMPLETE (ASSET_UPLOAD)" call *after* the binary
    # has already been delivered ("UPLOAD SUCCEEDED with no errors"), failing
    # the whole deploy even though the build is on its way into processing.
    # Retry to ride out the flake. The upload is effectively idempotent: if an
    # earlier attempt's binary did register, a later attempt reports it as a
    # duplicate, which we treat as success.
    testflight_upload_attempts = 0
    max_testflight_upload_attempts = 3
    begin
      testflight_upload_attempts += 1
      upload_to_testflight(
          api_key_path: "./ios/ios-fastlane-json-key.json",
          distribute_external: false,
      )
    rescue => e
      message = e.message.to_s

      if message.match?(/already been used|already exists|already been uploaded|redundant binary/i)
        UI.important("Build already present on App Store Connect; treating the TestFlight upload as successful (#{message.strip})")
      elsif testflight_upload_attempts < max_testflight_upload_attempts &&
            message.match?(/status code 5\d\d|internal server error|CHANGE UPLOAD STATE TO COMPLETE|Could not download\/upload|Error uploading ipa|timed out|connection reset/i)
        UI.error("TestFlight upload attempt #{testflight_upload_attempts}/#{max_testflight_upload_attempts} failed: #{message.strip}")
        UI.important("Retrying in 30s after a transient App Store Connect error...")
        sleep(30)
        retry
      else
        raise
      end
    end

    # A build that belongs to no beta group is installable by nobody: App Store
    # Connect leaves it at READY_FOR_BETA_TESTING and refuses tester invites with
    # NO_INSTALLABLE_BUILDS, so the deploy goes green and the build reaches no
    # one. pilot's own `groups:` cannot do this: it switches upload_to_testflight
    # to external-testing semantics even with distribute_external false, and then
    # fails on a missing Beta App Description. Assign over the API instead.
    # Deliberately fatal: a build nobody can install is not a successful deploy.
    sh("cd .. && node scripts/asc.mjs distribute --groups 'Kiroku Internal Beta Testers' --yes")

    begin
      # Read the Firebase app id out of the same plist the upload is keyed to,
      # rather than hardcoding it. A hardcoded id survives a bundle-id move
      # silently: the upload keeps succeeding against the retired Firebase app
      # and the dSYMs never reach the live one.
      gsp_path = "./ios/config/GoogleService-Info.prod.plist"
      crashlytics_app_id = sh(
        "/usr/libexec/PlistBuddy -c 'Print :GOOGLE_APP_ID' '#{gsp_path}'",
        log: false,
      ).strip
      UI.user_error!("GOOGLE_APP_ID missing from #{gsp_path}") if crashlytics_app_id.empty?

      upload_symbols_to_crashlytics(
        app_id: crashlytics_app_id,
        dsym_path: lane_context[SharedValues::DSYM_OUTPUT_PATH],
        gsp_path: gsp_path,
        binary_path: "./ios/Pods/FirebaseCrashlytics/upload-symbols",
      )
    rescue => e
      UI.error("Failed to upload dSYMs to Crashlytics: #{e.message}")
      UI.important("Continuing, TestFlight upload already succeeded. Symbolicate the affected build manually if needed.")
    end

  end

  desc "Move app to App Store Review"
  lane :production do
    # The binary was already built and uploaded to TestFlight by the staging
    # `beta` lane, so this lane must NOT rebuild or re-upload. It selects that
    # existing build by app_version/build_number and submits it for App Store
    # review; `skip_binary_upload: true` below is what tells deliver there is
    # nothing to upload.
    #
    # There is deliberately no TestFlight step here. This lane used to distribute
    # externally to a beta group first, which meant a Beta App Review round, a
    # Beta App Description, and an invite blast before anything reached the store.
    # External TestFlight is not part of the release path: testers install from
    # the App Store like everyone else. See BUNDLE_ID_MIGRATION.md, "External
    # TestFlight is deliberately absent".

    # VERSION will be set to the full build_number e.g. '1.0.92.0'
    build_number = ENV["VERSION"]
    # app_version needs to be set to the short version, without the last digit e.g. '1.0.92'
    app_version = build_number.rpartition(".")[0]

    # Leave alone anything Apple already has. If this version is waiting for or
    # in review, approved and pending release, or live, or any review
    # submission is open (e.g. one submitted by hand with IAPs attached),
    # deliver would re-edit it or cancel it. Skip instead: the lane still ends
    # green, so the Android job and the rest of the production deploy carry on.
    unless kiroku_submit_gate(app_version, build_number)
      message = "Skipped the App Store submission of #{build_number}; see the submit-gate output above. Nothing on App Store Connect was changed."
      UI.important(message)
      if ENV["GITHUB_ACTIONS"]
        puts("::warning title=App Store submission skipped::#{message}")
        File.write(ENV["GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"], "#{message}\n", mode: "a") if ENV["GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"]
      end
      next
    end

    deliver(
      api_key_path: "./ios/ios-fastlane-json-key.json",

      # Skip HTMl report verification
      force: true,

      build_number: build_number,
      app_version: app_version,

      # Merging to `production` submits for review. The build, metadata,
      # release notes, rating config and review information are all set by this
      # same deliver call, so there is nothing left to do by hand.
      #
      # FIRST SUBMISSION ONLY: in-app purchases must ride the app version or they
      # sit in Waiting for Review indefinitely, and it is unverified whether
      # deliver can attach them. Do the first one with
      #   node scripts/asc.mjs submit --iaps <ids> --yes
      # and let this lane take over from the second release on. The submit gate
      # above keeps the lane off that hand-made submission while it is open.
      submit_for_review: true,

      # Do NOT auto-release on approval, keep the approved build as Pending
      # Developer Release so we control the public launch date.
      automatic_release: false,

      # We need to upload metadata to upload the release notes which is required for each new version
      skip_metadata: false,

      # Define the metadata path
      metadata_path: "./fastlane/metadata",

      # Age-rating questionnaire (App Store Submission Kit §3, alcohol = Frequent/Intense → 17+)
      app_rating_config_path: "./fastlane/metadata/rating_config.json",

      # App Store review: demo sign-in account + contact (notes empty).
      app_review_information: kiroku_review_information,

      # We do not want to upload any screenshots
      skip_screenshots: true,

      # We do not have any binary to upload as it's already in TestFlight
      skip_binary_upload: true,

      # Never cancel a review from here. reject_if_possible cancels whatever
      # review submission is open for the platform, not just this version's,
      # so an older version in review (or IAPs riding it) would be pulled and
      # lose its place in the queue. The submit gate above already skips while
      # any submission is open, so the flag could only fire on a race, and
      # failing there beats withdrawing something unattended. To supersede a
      # version in review, withdraw it in App Store Connect and re-run the deploy.
      reject_if_possible: false,

      # We do not want to reset the ratings
      reset_ratings: false,

      # Precheck cannot check for in app purchases with the API key we use
      precheck_include_in_app_purchases: false,
      submission_information: {

        # We currently do not use idfa: https://developer.apple.com/app-store/user-privacy-and-data-use/
        add_id_info_uses_idfa: false,

        # We do not need any additional compliance
        export_compliance_compliance_required: false,

        # We do not use any encrpytion
        export_compliance_encryption_updated: false,
        export_compliance_app_type: nil,
        export_compliance_uses_encryption: false,
        export_compliance_is_exempt: false,
        export_compliance_contains_third_party_cryptography: false,
        export_compliance_contains_proprietary_cryptography: false,

        # We do not show any third party content
        content_rights_contains_third_party_content: false,

        # Indicate that our key has admin permissions
        content_rights_has_rights: true
      },
      release_notes: {
        'default' => ENV.fetch("TESTFLIGHT_CHANGELOG", "Improvements and bug fixes"),
        'en-US'   => ENV.fetch("TESTFLIGHT_CHANGELOG", "Improvements and bug fixes")
      }
    )

  end

  desc "Capture App Store screenshots across the device + locale matrix"
  lane :screenshots do
    ENV["ENVFILE"] = ".env.production"

    # Demo credentials must be exported in the shell before invoking the lane -
    # the same env vars the :production lane already uses for App Store Review.
    UI.user_error!("APPLE_DEMO_EMAIL not set") unless ENV["APPLE_DEMO_EMAIL"]
    UI.user_error!("APPLE_DEMO_PASSWORD not set") unless ENV["APPLE_DEMO_PASSWORD"]

    # Generate the KirokuUITests target inside ios/kiroku.xcodeproj at runtime.
    # The resulting pbxproj diff is intentionally throwaway and must not be
    # committed, committing it bumps objectVersion via Xcode 26 and breaks
    # `bundle exec pod install` for everyone. See the script header and
    # contributingGuides/SCREENSHOTS.md for the full story.
    # Fastlane runs lanes with cwd = ./fastlane, so step up one directory.
    sh "bundle exec ruby ../scripts/setup-screenshots-test-target.rb"

    # The kiroku target embeds a watchOS companion whose WKCompanionAppBundleIdentifier
    # is the real device bundle id, but iphonesimulator builds resolve the phone
    # app to a different simulator-only id, so the OS refuses the watch app's
    # "no matching companion installed" install. Same fix the watch capture job
    # uses; see the script header for the full story. Throwaway, not committed.
    sh "bundle exec ruby ../scripts/set-simulator-bundle-id.rb"

    capture_ios_screenshots         # reads Snapfile

    # snapshot writes flat files, <locale>/<device>-<shot>.png, but both
    # scripts/verify-captured-screenshots.mjs and
    # scripts/ingest-store-screenshots.mjs consume device subfolders,
    # <locale>/<device>/<shot>.png. Normalize here so every consumer of the
    # capture (CI verify gate, local ingest) sees one layout. The shot names
    # all start with two digits (01_Home .. 07_Settings), which is what the
    # lookahead splits on; device names may themselves contain hyphens
    # ("iPad Pro 13-inch (M5)") so a plain split on "-" would mangle them.
    require 'fileutils'
    screenshots_root = File.expand_path('screenshots/ios', __dir__)
    Dir.glob(File.join(screenshots_root, '*')).select { |p| File.directory?(p) }.each do |locale_dir|
      Dir.glob(File.join(locale_dir, '*.png')).each do |png|
        base = File.basename(png, '.png')
        match = base.match(/\A(?<device>.+)-(?<shot>\d{2}_.+)\z/)
        next unless match
        device_dir = File.join(locale_dir, match[:device])
        FileUtils.mkdir_p(device_dir)
        FileUtils.mv(png, File.join(device_dir, "#{match[:shot]}.png"))
      end
    end
    # frame_screenshots(white: true) # uncomment once you've installed `frameit`
  end

  desc "Push App Store listing metadata to the editable ASC draft WITHOUT submitting for review"
  lane :upload_metadata do
    # NOTE: deliver's `verify_only` flag validates only the *binary* package, not
    # metadata, so it cannot dry-run a listing. The faithful validation is to
    # upload the metadata to the editable App Store version with
    # submit_for_review:false. This writes to the ASC draft (fully editable, NOT
    # a submission) and surfaces any field-length / allowed-value errors.

    deliver(
      api_key_path: "./ios/ios-fastlane-json-key.json",
      force: true,
      run_precheck_before_submit: false,

      # Target the existing editable version. Override with VERSION if needed.
      app_version: (ENV["VERSION"] ? ENV["VERSION"].rpartition(".")[0] : nil),

      submit_for_review: false,
      skip_binary_upload: true,
      skip_screenshots: true,
      skip_metadata: false,
      metadata_path: "./fastlane/metadata",
      app_rating_config_path: "./fastlane/metadata/rating_config.json",
      app_review_information: kiroku_review_information,
      precheck_include_in_app_purchases: false
    )
  end

end
